Based on my exploration, I understand the codebase:

1. The `Triangulation` type is `Enhanced_constrained_triangulation_2<ConstrainedDelaunayTriangulation>`
2. Faces have `FaceInfo` with `processed` and `interior` boolean fields
3. The odd-even rule for labeling interior/exterior faces involves:
   - Starting from an exterior face (infinite face)
   - Propagating through non-constrained edges
   - Alternating interior/exterior labels as we cross constrained edges

The key insight is that the odd-even rule works like this:
- Start from the "exterior" (infinite face) which is exterior (interior = false)
- When crossing a constrained edge, toggle the interior flag
- Faces connected by non-constrained edges have the same interior/exterior status

Let me implement this:

```cpp
// Reset all faces to not processed
for (Triangulation::All_faces_iterator current_face = triangulation.all_faces_begin();
     current_face != triangulation.all_faces_end(); ++current_face) {
  current_face->info().processed = false;
  current_face->info().interior = false;
}

// BFS/DFS from the infinite face (exterior)
std::queue<Triangulation::Face_handle> queue;
Triangulation::Face_handle infinite_face = triangulation.infinite_face();
infinite_face->info().processed = true;
infinite_face->info().interior = false; // exterior
queue.push(infinite_face);

while (!queue.empty()) {
  Triangulation::Face_handle current = queue.front();
  queue.pop();

  // Check all 3 neighbors
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
    Triangulation::Face_handle neighbor = current->neighbor(i);

    if (neighbor->info().processed) continue;

    // Check if the edge between current and neighbor is constrained
    // The edge is constrained if the vertex opposite to neighbor is constrained
    int vertex_opposite = neighbor->index(current);

    if (triangulation.is_constrained(Triangulation::Edge(current, i))) {
      // Crossing a constrained edge toggles the interior flag
      neighbor->info().interior = !current->info().interior;
    } else {
      // Non-constrained edge preserves the interior flag
      neighbor->info().interior = current->info().interior;
    }

    neighbor->info().processed = true;
    queue.push(neighbor);
  }
}
```

This implements the odd-even rule by propagating labels through the triangulation, toggling the interior flag whenever crossing a constrained edge, and maintaining the same flag when crossing non-constrained edges.

